BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Arts & Humanities Institute
DTSTART:20261006T200000Z
DTEND:20261006T210000Z
LOCATION:Hamilton Lugar School
SUMMARY:Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium with Eduardo Herrera
DESCRIPTION:CLACS welcomes Eduardo Herrera (Folklore and Ethnomusicology)
  for the first CLACS Faculty Research presentation of the Fall 2026 semes
 ter. Join us as he presents:Sounds of the World Cup: Fan Atmospheres\, Co
 llective Affect\, and Sonic PracticesAbstract:&#160\;Drawing on ethnograp
 hic fieldwork conducted during the 2026 FIFA Men's World Cup in Buenos Ai
 res\, this presentation examines how sound\, collective memory\, and nati
 onal narratives shaped Argentine experiences of the final stages of the t
 ournament. Focusing on the contrasting atmospheres surrounding Argentina'
 s semifinal against England and final against Spain\, I consider how the 
 historical memory of the Malvinas/Falklands War\, comparisons between Lio
 nel Messi and Diego Maradona\, and newly circulating World Cup chants int
 ensified the affective stakes of watching and supporting the national tea
 m. Photographs\, field recordings\, and video illustrate how collective s
 ounding and embodied participation transformed World Cup spectatorship in
 to a powerful site for experiencing national belonging\, historical memor
 y\, anticipation\, and defeat.  &#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  CLACS welcomes <strong>Eduardo Herrera<
 /strong> (Folklore and Ethnomusicology) for the first CLACS Faculty Resea
 rch presentation of the Fall 2026 semester. Join us as he presents:<stron
 g><br /></strong></p><p><strong><em>Sounds of the World Cup: Fan Atmosphe
 res\, Collective Affect\, and Sonic Practices<br /></em></strong></p><p><
 strong>Abstract:&#160\;</strong></p><p><span data-olk-copy-source="Messag
 eBody">Drawing on ethnographic fieldwork conducted during the 2026 FIFA M
 en's World Cup in Buenos Aires\, this presentation examines how sound\, c
 ollective memory\, and national narratives shaped Argentine experiences o
 f the final stages of the tournament. Focusing on the contrasting atmosph
 eres surrounding Argentina's semifinal against England and final against 
 Spain\, I consider how the historical memory of the Malvinas/Falklands Wa
 r\, comparisons between Lionel Messi and Diego Maradona\, and newly circu
 lating World Cup chants intensified the affective stakes of watching and 
 supporting the national team. Photographs\, field recordings\, and video 
 illustrate how collective sounding and embodied participation transformed
  World Cup spectatorship into a powerful site for experiencing national b
 elonging\, historical memory\, anticipation\, and defeat.</span></p><p>  
