SUMMARY:Memory Transformed Symposium: Materials of Heritage in Jewish Eas
 tern European Life
DESCRIPTION:Join the Borns Jewish Studies Program for the opening symposi
 um of Memory Transformed: Materials of Heritage in Jewish Eastern Europea
 n Life\, a day of scholarship\, art\, and music exploring how Eastern Eur
 opean Jewish culture is remembered and continues to shape contemporary ar
 tistic production.11:00 a.m. — Complimentary Brunch at the Tudor Room  
 Attendees are welcome to join us for a brunch before the symposium begins
 . Brunch is open to all Memory Transformed attendees.12:30–3:00 p.m. �
 � SymposiumDogwood Room\, Indiana Memorial Union  The symposium will feat
 ure two panels examining Jewish material culture\, music\, memory\, and t
 he cultural afterlives of objects associated with Eastern European Jewish
  life. Scholars and artists will explore how recordings\, photographs\, b
 ooks\, memorials\, and other cultural objects preserve knowledge\, shape 
 memory\, and inspire contemporary cultural production.Panel 1Judith Berks
 onPatterns of Improvisation in Live Cantorial Recordings\, 1950–1980Jer
 emiah LockwoodPlastinkes and idisher rekords: Towards a History of Jewish
  Sacred Pop Music as Material CultureDov-Ber KerlerFrom the New to the Ol
 d World and Vice Versa: Contrafacts\, Adaptation and Radical ReworkingPan
 el 2Halina GoldbergMaterial Culture\, Memory\, and the Making of a Museum
  and Digital Project on Jewish ŁódźNicolette van den BogerdComposing J
 ewish Musical Material Culture After the HolocaustJennifer McComasTransfo
 rmations of Material and Memory in Ruth Weinberg's Shtetl PortfolioModera
 tors  Joanna Niżyńska  Sergio Ospina-Romero  The symposium is part of t
 he larger Memory Transformed program taking place throughout the day on S
 unday\, October 11\, including brunch\, an afternoon reception and option
 al guided museum tours at the Eskenazi Museum of Art\, and an evening con
 cert at Auer Hall.Free and open to the public. Registration is encouraged
 .
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Join the Borns Jewish Studies Program f
 or the opening symposium of Memory Transformed: Materials of Heritage in 
 Jewish Eastern European Life\, a day of scholarship\, art\, and music exp
 loring how Eastern European Jewish culture is remembered and continues to
  shape contemporary artistic production.</p><p><strong>11:00 a.m. — Com
 plimentary Brunch at the Tudor Room</strong><br />  Attendees are welcome
  to join us for a brunch before the symposium begins. Brunch is open to a
 ll Memory Transformed attendees.</p><p><strong>12:30–3:00 p.m. — Symp
 osium</strong><br /><strong>Dogwood Room\, Indiana Memorial Union</strong
 ></p><p>  The symposium will feature two panels examining Jewish material
  culture\, music\, memory\, and the cultural afterlives of objects associ
 ated with Eastern European Jewish life. Scholars and artists will explore
  how recordings\, photographs\, books\, memorials\, and other cultural ob
 jects preserve knowledge\, shape memory\, and inspire contemporary cultur
 al production.</p><p><strong>Panel 1</strong></p><p><strong>Judith Berkso
