X-SOURCE:IU Arts & Humanities Institute
DTSTART:20261023T173000Z
DTEND:20261023T181500Z
SUMMARY:Master Class in Documentary Filmmaking with Natalie Pattillo
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, October 231:30 - 2:15 PM  In-Person\, Lindley Hall 3
 28  In recognition of Domestic Violence Awareness Month\, join award-winn
 ing documentary filmmaker Natalie Pattillo for a master class in document
 ary filmmaking.  This practical and interactive conversation will focus o
 n documentary filmmaking\, ethical storytelling\, working with vulnerable
  subjects\, survivor-centered narratives\, and the responsibilities of fi
 lmmakers telling stories involving trauma and violence.Participants will 
 come away with tools for:Story Development: Finding and developing a stor
 y with stakes\, tension\, and emotion while staying grounded in your proj
 ect's "North Star."  Anatomy of a Scene &amp\; Thinking Visually: Unpacki
 ng the choices behind creating impactful scenes and adapting when things 
 don't go as planned.  Interviewing\, Trust\, and Connection: Building tru
 st with film participants\, asking thoughtful questions\, listening close
 ly\, and making space for complexity or unexpected threads.  Ethics\, Bou
 ndaries\, and Care: Navigating consent\, safety\, power\, responsibility\
 , sensitive topics\, and everyone on set's well-being (including your own
 !).  Creative Collaboration: Working with a co-director\, cinematographer
 s\, editor\, sound team\, and music composer to create a cohesive film an
 d being okay with learning on the fly.  Funding\, Distribution\, and Impa
 ct: Finding ways to fund the work (challenging but not impossible!)\, def
 ining what success looks like for you/your film\, and getting a film to c
 ommunities that will use it as a tool for powerful impact.    —** You c
 an also join Natalie Pattillo on Friday\, October 23 at 2:30 PM for a Dom
 estic Violence\, Storytelling\, and Social Change Workshop (https://event
 s.iu.edu/kinsey/event/2383527-domestic-violence-storytelling-and-social-c
 hange). Natalie's documentary film\, And So I Stayed will screen at the I
 U Cinema on Saturday\, October 24 at 7:00 PM. **Natalie Pattillo is an aw
 ard-winning documentary filmmaker\, writer and journalist whose work expl
 ores education\, child care access\, gender-based violence\, food and cul
 ture. Her work has appeared in The New York Times\, MSNBC\, VICE\, New Yo
 rk Magazine\, Al Jazeera America\, Salon and beyond. In 2020\, she receiv
 ed the Media Award from the National Coalition Against Domestic Violence.
  She was also named a Faculty News Champion by the Center for Community N
 ews at the University of Vermont for her work supporting student journali
 sm and rebuilding local news.  Natalie was the co-director\, writer and p
 roducer of And So I Stayed (https://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.co
 m/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2F2021%2F06%2F11%2Fmovies%2Fdomestic
 -violence-law.html&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402
 135b208def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C6392170923
 79681366%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAw
 MCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sd
 ata=%2Fi1Tl7bk%2F8Acy%2B5RDgCzQCc%2F7gSL9gR5STvkaDCSvoQ%3D&amp\;reserved=
 0)\, an award-winning documentary about survivors of domestic violence wh
 o were incarcerated for acts of survival. The film has been featured by T
 he New York Times\, the Chicago Tribune and ABC News.  She is currently w
 orking on Writing for My Mother (https://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlo
 ok.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.documentaries.org%2Ffilms%2Fwriting-for-my-
 mother%2F&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b208de
 f48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379713731%
 7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiO
 iJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=HJDUr
 Vkh4JRuRMGml0LyUARQNxyWimVxZrLDyPnKf38%3D&amp\;reserved=0)\, a documentar
 y and book project exploring literacy in the United States\, the communit
 ies that have been excluded from educational opportunities and what becom
 es possible in people's lives and for our democracy when those barriers a
 re broken down.  Natalie lives in Providence\, Rhode Island\, with her hu
 sband and three children. In her free time\, she can usually be found gar
 dening\, knitting\, reading\, or taking unhurried walks.AND SO I STAYEDis
  an&#160\;eye-opening and award-winning documentary about three survivors
  of abuse fighting for their lives and how the legal system betrayed them
 .&#160\;These women paid a steep price with long prison sentences\, lost 
 time with loved ones\, and painful memories. Formerly incarcerated surviv
 or-advocate Kim Dadou Brown\, who met her wife while incarcerated\, is a 
 driving force in the passage of New York's Domestic Violence Survivors Ju
 stice Act (DVSJA)\, a new law meant to prevent survivors from receiving h
 arsh prison sentences for their acts of survival. Nikki Addimando\, a mot
 her of two young children\, suffered the consequences when a judge didn't
  follow the law's guidelines. Tanisha Davis\, a single mother who was rip
 ped away from her son in 2013\, is hopeful the new law is her way out of 
 a harsh prison sentence.AND SO I STAYED aims to hold the legal system and
  public accountable by exposing the unjust misogyny and racism embedded i
 n these power structures while also portraying the resilience\, hopes\, a
 nd dreams of survivors and their loved ones.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Fri
 day\, October 23</strong></h5><h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>1:
 30 - 2:15 PM</strong></h5><p style="text-align: center\;">  In-Person\, L
 indley Hall 328</p><p>  In recognition of Domestic Violence Awareness Mon
 th\, join award-winning documentary filmmaker Natalie Pattillo for a mast
 er class in documentary filmmaking.</p><p>  This practical and interactiv
 e conversation will focus on documentary filmmaking\, ethical storytellin
 g\, working with vulnerable subjects\, survivor-centered narratives\, and
  the responsibilities of filmmakers telling stories involving trauma and 
 violence.</p><p dir="ltr"><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><em><s
 trong>Participants will come away with tools for</strong></em>:</span></p
