  work (challenging but not impossible!)\, defining what success looks lik
 e for you/your film\, and getting a film to communities that will use it 
 as a tool for powerful impact.  </li></ul><p style="text-align: center\;"
 >  —</p><p style="text-align: center\;"><em>**</em> You can also join N
 atalie Pattillo on Friday\, October 23 at 2:30 PM for a <a href="https://
 events.iu.edu/kinsey/event/2383527-domestic-violence-storytelling-and-soc
 ial-change" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Domestic Violence\, Storytelli
 ng\, and Social Change Workshop</a>. Natalie's documentary film\, <a href
 ="https://events.iu.edu/kinsey/event/2383529-screening-of-and-so-i-stayed
 -with-filmmaker" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>And So I Stayed</em><
 /a> will screen at the IU Cinema on Saturday\, October 24 at 7:00 PM. **<
 /p><p><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><strong>Natalie Pattillo</
 strong> is an award-winning documentary filmmaker\, writer and journalist
  whose work explores education\, child care access\, gender-based violenc
 e\, food and culture. Her work has appeared in The New York Times\, MSNBC
 \, VICE\, New York Magazine\, Al Jazeera America\, Salon and beyond. In 2
 020\, she received the Media Award from the National Coalition Against Do
 mestic Violence. She was also named a Faculty News Champion by the Center
  for Community News at the University of Vermont for her work supporting 
 student journalism and rebuilding local news.</span></p><p>  Natalie was 
 the co-director\, writer and producer of <em><a title="Original URL: http
 s://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/11/movies/domestic-violence-law.html. Click o
 r tap if you trust this link." href="https://nam12.safelinks.protection.o
 utlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2F2021%2F06%2F11%2Fmovies%2
